On Sat, 12 Jul 2003, Kelledin wrote:

>> I just looked at newegg.com, and they have a Powercolor PCI
>> 9000 for US$89 (plus various 7000 and 7500 PCI models for
>> less). Wonder how much of a performance gain that would have
>> over the 7500... ;-}
>
>Well, considering that the Radeon 7500 PCI cards often go for 
>about US$75 on eBay, it might be worth the extra 20% cost. ;)
>
>BTW, does anyone know of any significant differences between the 
>Powercolor cards and the "Built by ATI" cards?  Any 
>success/failure reports getting them to work on Alphas?  It 
>would be nice to know if I can safely save a few $$$ by going 
>with Powercolor...

Generally speaking, "Powered by ATI" cards are 10% or more slower 
clocked than their "Built by ATI" counterparts.  Some cards are 
the same speed however as ATI has provided some of their boards 
to 3rd party manufacturers to kick-start their production lines 
so to speak, at least that's what I've heard around the net 
somewhere.

Aside from the performance difference, the cards may or may not 
function identically.  Early reports using 4.2.1 and lower 
releases of XFree86, were that some powered-by-ATI boards would 
not work or would have some problems that varied, while other 
cards worked perfectly.  This was attributed to how closely the 
3rd party boards followed ATI's reference designs, which of 
course were used to write the open source drivers with.

I haven't heard any problem reports about Powered-by-ATI boards 
for 4.3.0 that I recall however, so it's possible any driver 
problems may have been worked out since then.

Also note, all of this 'research' so to speak, is all Intel x86 
based and not Alpha based, so it may not hold true for Alpha.

My recommendation, is to purchase an official Built by ATI board, 
to both:

1) Maximize the chances of it working
2) Maximize the performance
3) Greatly improve the chances that someone can investigate, 
   debug, troubleshoot, and fix any problems you encounter, 
   because the majority of developers have Built By ATI hardware
   it seems.

Also, I would get a "works for me" test result FIRST from someone
else with the same computer hardware and video board you're
considering buying if at all possible.  If your card doesn't 
work, it will likely be very difficult to get someone to fix it, 
as it'd likely require someone with both the same Alpha machine, 
and the same Radeon PCI card.  It's a rarity just to find somoene 
who has Radeon PCI hardware, let alone also using it on Alpha, 
and also being an X developer, and also having time to allot to 
investigation.  ;o)

Hope this helps.


-- 
