IBM finally dropped the other shoe...

The 970 Chip (Apple's G5) will power a new series of "low end" super 
powerful Linux servers.

http://www.eweek.com/article2/0,3959,1197450,00.asp

"The ULE models, which will run Linux and IBM's AIX OS, will ship in 2U 
two-way and 4U four-way configurations. A base configuration of the 4U 
is expected to cost less than $3,500, sources said.
...
With the upcoming PowerPC 970- and GPUL2-based ULE products, IBM will 
stress better performance than Xeon-based servers, 32- and 64-bit 
compatibility with no migration costs or penalties, and linear price 
scaling from two-way to four-way systems. Against Itanium and Itanium 2 
servers, IBM will promote the ULE as cheaper, less power-hungry, cooler 
and easier to set up."


